y matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=4,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=4.$" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441350 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441353 1069 "q-106752" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441353 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441353 1072 #uuid "5d565601-7981-50a3-bc57-df1008cb679e" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441353 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441349 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441354 1069 "q-75115" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441354 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $B?$\n\nI. $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441354 1072 #uuid "73ab53ad-67cd-53a7-b27f-c766bd840711" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441354 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441348 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441355 1069 "q-32611" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441355 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $B?$\n\nI. $B=[\\begin{aligned}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441355 1072 #uuid "8cd9aa05-4a21-5975-bf09-f09975b43169" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441355 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains numbers different from $1.$\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441351 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441356 1069 "q-106772" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441356 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441356 1072 #uuid "a069a9c2-f0e9-5f4e-8079-7e889bf03a9b" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true] [17592186441356 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=5,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=5.$" 13194139534858 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-1fb42e2c-aa24-5f95-88b6-df41338ccd5f" 17592186441322 "answer-238ca86a-9d68-5724-a14e-22dd68af5c77" 17592186441323 "answer-289d8b11-5b33-5df3-83e4-bfbab449cfa4" 17592186441324 "answer-374fcecf-456e-59e1-810b-f5c42633df35" 17592186441325 "answer-3f014fff-e379-5421-b2b4-49d5a54f23a1" 17592186441326 "answer-48377761-7467-51b7-97c9-f6247717ea78" 17592186441327 "answer-4c31da62-609b-5d8a-8854-0a6fa3314fc7" 1759